{
  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-103990",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "First, we convert the integrand into a sum of partial fractions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x+15}{(x-3)(x+3)} & =\\frac{A}{x-3}+\\frac{B}{x+3},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\nx+15=A(x+3)+B(x-3).\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=-3,$ we can eliminate $A$ and solve for $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3+15 & =A(-3+3)+B(-3-3) \\\\ 12 & =-6B \\\\ B & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, plugging in $x=3,$ we can eliminate $B$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3+15 & =A(3+3)+B(3-3) \\\\ 18 & =6A \\\\ A & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x+15}{(x-3)(x+3)} & =\\frac{3}{x-3}-\\frac{2}{x+3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we integrate, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{x+15}{(x-3)(x+3)}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{3}{x-3}-\\frac{2}{x+3})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{3}{x-3}\\,\\text{d}x-\\int \\frac{2}{x+3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-3|-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+3|+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, $a=3,b=-2,$ and $a+b=3-2=1.$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
